<Docs title="Architecture & deployment"><div class="doc-title"><span class="section-kicker">ARCHITECTURE / OPERATE</span><h1>Choose where the boundary lives.</h1><p>Run the authorizer in-process, next to your application, or behind a shared HTTP interface.</p></div><h2>Embedded Rust</h2><p>agentguard-core::Authorizer evaluates in-process. Your code handles trusted identity, supplied entities, audit logging, and enforcement. agentguard_server::build_router returns an Axum router and shared state when you want the HTTP surface inside your service.</p><p>The <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/tree/master/examples/rust-embedder">Rust example</a> demonstrates router composition. build_router already defines /access/v1 routes; nesting that returned router under /access/v1, as the example currently does, adds another prefix. Merge it or choose the external path deliberately. The standalone binary uses the normal /access/v1/evaluation path.</p><h2>Local sidecar</h2><Snippet title="Loopback server" code={'agentguard-server --listen tcp://127.0.0.1:8443 --store .agentguard'}/><p>Use a local process boundary for a non-Rust application. Plain HTTP is suitable only within your trusted local boundary. The optional gRPC listener is plaintext and explicitly limited to loopback.</p><h2>Shared PDP</h2><p>A shared HTTP service requires authentication, TLS, protected policy storage, and a durable audit destination. Listener::Tls uses a URL shaped as <code>tls://127.0.0.1:8443?cert=server.pem&amp;key=server.key</code>. Protect health and metrics endpoints at the network boundary; API-key middleware applies to decision routes.</p><p>API-key authentication is implemented in AuthConfig::ApiKey and AuthLayer. The standalone binary currently has an invalid clap requires reference named auth_apikey on --auth-key-file. Debug builds can panic while parsing; do not treat the binary flags as a verified remote deployment recipe. Embedders can construct ServerConfig directly. Review and validate your selected deployment path before production use.</p><h2>State and policy lifecycle</h2><p>Persist the schema, policies, audit log, .chainid sidecar, and chain secret with suitable access controls. The authorizer loads its policies at construction. Current filesystem/SIGHUP reload handling invalidates caches but does not replace the loaded authorizer: restart after policy changes.</p><p>Validate changes first and replay representative allow/deny requests. Restoring an earlier file bundle is a rollback only after the serving process loads it. No Kubernetes manifest or supported Helm deployment is shipped with the examples.</p><h2>Cache and telemetry wiring</h2><p>Standalone startup does not enable the optional core decision cache or construct an OTLP sink. Embedding APIs expose those facilities; wire and verify them explicitly. Environment variables documented for a library do not automatically activate it in a binary.</p><h2>Health and shutdown</h2><p>/healthz reports liveness; /readyz requires policies and configured audit state. Neither proves that policies grant the intended permissions. The server handles SIGTERM/SIGINT with a bounded drain; exercise lifecycle behavior with your process supervisor.</p><p>Sources: <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-server/src/server.rs">server lifecycle</a>, <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-server/src/listener.rs">listener configuration</a>, <a href="https://github.com/sachncs/agent-guard/blob/master/crates/agentguard-server/src/main.rs">binary parser</a>.</p></Docs>
